a. Assuming a 1Gbps network bandwidth, the transmission delay for a message of size S bits is:

T = S / B
T = S / 1Gbps
T = S / 10^9

For the proposed architecture, four types of MPI messages are used:
- Neighbour status request
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Scaling with number of charging nodes N: O(1) per message, O(N) total communication
    - Each charging node communicates with at most four immediate neighbours in the 2D mesh. Consequently, increasing N does not increase the transmission delay of an individual request
    - However, the total number of neighbour requests increases proportionally with the number of charging nodes

- Neighbour status response
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Scaling with number of charging nodes N: O(1) per message, O(N) total communication
    - Each neighbour returns its utilisation and charging-port status, with the total number of responses increases with the number of charging nodes

- Overload alert
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Scaling with number of charging nodes N: O(1) per message, O(N) worst-case total communication
    - An overloaded charging node sends an alert to the base station
    - The number of possible alerts increases with N

- Redirection instruction
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Scaling with number of charging nodes N: O(1) per message, O(N) worst-case total communication
    - The base station sends a redirection instruction when an alternative charging node is required.
    - The number of redirection messages depends on the number of overload events. In the worst case, this can increase proportionally with N

Overall result
Increasing the number of charging nodes does not increase the theoretical transmission delay of an individual MPI message, assuming fixed message sizes and a fixed 1 Gbps link.

Instead, it increases the number of messages and aggregate communication workload. For the 2D mesh, neighbour communication therefore has O(1) per-message transmission delay and O(N) aggregate communication complexity.

b. For the proposed architecture, increasing the number of base stations can reduce the overall communication and processing delay by distributing charging nodes and their messages across multiple servers:
- Neighbour status request
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Increasing the number of base stations does not directly reduce the transmission delay of an individual request because the message size and link bandwidth remain unchanged
    However, distributing charging nodes across multiple base stations can reduce the communication and processing workload handled by each server
    This can reduce queuing and processing delays when many charging nodes send requests simultaneously

- Neighbour status response
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Increasing the number of base stations does not change the theoretical transmission delay of an individual response
    - Responses can be distributed across multiple servers, reducing the likelihood of congestion and server-side queuing
    - Therefore, the overall response time may decrease under high communication loads

- Overload alert
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Increasing the number of base stations can distribute overload alerts across multiple servers
    - This reduces the number of alerts processed by each individual server and can reduce queuing and processing delays
    - However, the transmission delay of each individual alert remains unchanged
- Redirection instruction
    - Transmission delay per message: T = S / 10^9
    - Multiple base stations can process redirection requests in parallel, reducing server-side processing and queuing delays
    - The transmission delay of an individual redirection instruction remains unchanged because the message size and network bandwidth are fixed
    - The benefit is therefore mainly from reduced processing and congestion rather than reduced transmission time

Overall result
Increasing the number of base stations does not reduce the theoretical transmission delay of an individual MPI message, assuming fixed message sizes and 1 Gbps links.

Instead, multiple base stations distribute the communication and processing workload across servers. This can reduce queuing, congestion and processing delays, particularly as the number of charging nodes increases.

Therefore, increasing the number of base stations can reduce the overall communication delay, but the individual transmission delay remains T = S / 10^9.